curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186119505 1100 "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1$." 13194139535673 false] [17592186119505 1100 "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186119505 1145 "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {\\mid y''(x) \\mid} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2}, \\mid y^{″}(x)|= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(x) &= (\\mid 6x\\begin{vmatrix})/([1 + (3x^{2})^{2}]^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid 6x\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9x^{4})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(-1) &= (\\mid 6(-1)\\begin{vmatrix})/((1 + 9(-1)^{4})^{3/2}) \\\\ = (\\mid - 6\\end{vmatrix})/((1 + 9)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10^{3/2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 false] [17592186119505 1145 "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {|y''(x)|} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and $y''(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2},\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|6x|}{{[1+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6x|}{{(1+9{x}^{4})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(-1) & =\\frac{|6(-1)|}{{(1+9(-1)^{4})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6|}{{(1+9)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{10}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186119506 1100 "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{ (1+\\cos^2 t)^3 } }, \\quad t\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139535673 true] [17592186119506 1100 "The signed curvature of the plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle$ is given by\n$k (t) = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+\\cos^2 t)^3}}, \\quad t\\neq 0$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139535673 false] [17592186119506 1145 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(t) &= (1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - 0 \\cdot \\cos t)/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-\\sin t)/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-sin ⁡ t)/(\\sqrt{(1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 false] [17592186119506 1145 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186119685 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360497 13194139535673 true] [17592186119685 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360525 13194139535673 true] [17592186119685 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360532 13194139535673 true] [17592186119685 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360539 13194139535673 true] [17592186119686 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360546 13194139535673 true] [17592186119686 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360553 13