${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186120426 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535673 true] [17592186120426 1100 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩$ at $t = 0$." 13194139535673 false] [17592186120426 1100 "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186120426 1145 "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535673 true] [17592186360483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360484 13